# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Between Jobs API -- environment variables
#
#   cp .env.example .env     then fill in the two REQUIRED values below
#
# Plain KEY=value lines, one per line: no quotes and no `export` (Docker's --env-file
# reads a line literally, so quotes would become part of the value). A line that starts
# with `# NAME=` is an optional setting shown with its default; delete the `# ` to use it.
# Optional settings that have a default are commented out on purpose: a blank `NAME=`
# is an empty value, not an unset one.
#
# Every variable the API (src/) and the operator scripts (scripts/) read is listed
# here; tests/test_env_example.py fails if the code reads one that is missing. The web
# app and the browser extension have their own files: web/.env.example and
# extension/.env.example.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# --- REQUIRED: Supabase ---
# The API refuses to start without both of these.
#
# Hosted project: dashboard -> Project Settings -> API (the project URL, and the
# service_role secret).
# Local stack (`supabase start`): run `supabase status -o env`; API_URL and
# SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are the two values (the URL is http://127.0.0.1:54321).
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# The service-role key bypasses row-level security: this backend is a trusted server
# (channels and the web app talk to this API, never to Postgres directly), so it has to.
# Treat it like a password. NEVER the anon/publishable key, never in the web app or the
# extension, never in git.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- OPTIONAL: the resume engine (forge-engines) ---
# Base URL of the HTTP service that generates resumes and cover letters. Default
# http://localhost:5682. The engine is NOT part of this repository yet -- without one
# running, resume and cover-letter generation, the Tailor panel, the gap interview,
# the header preview and interview practice fail with a retryable PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE
# error ("Couldn't reach the resume engine"); see README.md, "What works today". No
# credential goes here: the engine is handed the user's own LLM key per call.
# FORGE_ENGINES_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5682

# --- OPTIONAL: the LaTeX compile service (latex-service/, its own Dockerfile, port 5700) ---
# Where the API sends LaTeX to be compiled to a PDF. Default http://localhost:5700, which
# is only right when both run on the same machine -- set it when the API runs in a
# container or on a platform. /health reports it as `unreachable` until it is right.
# LATEX_SERVICE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5700

# How many PDF compiles this API process sends to the LaTeX service at once (default 2).
# Pdflatex is CPU-bound, so a small number keeps every compile quick; the rest wait their
# turn for up to 30 seconds, then the request fails with a retryable "renderer busy" error.
# A positive whole number; anything else stops the API from starting. Raise it only if the
# LaTeX service has the cores for it.
# LATEX_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- OPTIONAL: request limits ---
# The largest request body the API accepts, in bytes (default 1048576, 1 MiB). A bigger
# body -- declared up front or streamed past the limit -- is refused with a 413
# PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE before it is read into memory. Nearly every route here takes a small
# JSON document, so the default is generous; the one exception is the resume file upload
# (POST /profile/import-document), which has its own fixed 5 MiB cap whatever this is set
# to. A positive whole number, anything else stops the API from starting.
# MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES=1048576
# The per-user rate limits on the routes that spend money or heavy compute (discovery,
# resume generation, company research, ...) are not environment settings: they are a
# table in src/between_jobs/api/rate_limits.py, enforced in the database. A user over a
# limit gets a 429 RATE_LIMITED with a Retry-After header.

# --- OPTIONAL: Telegram bridge ---
# Set BOTH of the next two values, or leave BOTH empty to run without Telegram (web
# only). With neither set the webhook and the Integrations page's Telegram card are
# off; with only one set the server refuses to start.
# Create a bot with @BotFather (/newbot) and paste its token here.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Shared secret Telegram echoes back on every webhook call so this app can tell a real
# Telegram update from a forged POST to the same URL. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Register the webhook with Telegram once the API has a public HTTPS URL (any tunnel
# works for local development). Export both values in your shell (the script does not
# read this file) and run:
#   python scripts/set_telegram_webhook.py --url https://<your-public-host> --dry-run
#   python scripts/set_telegram_webhook.py --url https://<your-public-host>
# A bot has ONE webhook: use a separate bot for dev and for prod.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The bot's public username (with or without the @), shown on the web app's Integrations
# page so people know which bot to send their link code to. Cosmetic: without it the page
# says "this server's Telegram bot". Ignored when Telegram is off.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=

# --- OPTIONAL: the web app's public address ---
# The https address people open the web app at, for example https://between-jobs.tech. The
# Telegram bot's /privacy and /learn messages use it to link to the website's Privacy Policy
# and to the pages where the setup steps are done. Left unset, the same messages are sent
# without a link and say the full policy is on the website.
# An https address with a host (a path is allowed) and nothing else: no user name or password,
# no query string, no fragment. Anything else stops the API from starting; the error names
# this setting and never its value.
# WEB_APP_URL=

# --- OPTIONAL: Gmail drafts and reply checking ---
# Not needed to boot the app. Only the Gmail connect and callback routes, the route that
# creates a Gmail draft (push-to-gmail) and the Gmail reply-checker worker read these, at
# request or tick time. Leave them unset and those routes answer SETUP_REQUIRED and the
# reply checker does nothing; everything else is unaffected. All three are needed together.
# Google Cloud Console -> APIs & services -> Credentials -> OAuth 2.0 Client ID (type:
# Web application); enable the plain "Gmail API" for the project. The scopes requested
# are gmail.compose (create a draft) and gmail.readonly (read a thread the app already
# knows, for reply checking). The API never calls Gmail's send endpoint: the human
# sends. Treat the client secret like a password.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Must exactly match one of the "Authorized redirect URIs" registered on that same OAuth
# client. The route is /oauth/gmail/callback on the API's public origin; this is the
# local-development value for the API on port 8012.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8012/oauth/gmail/callback

# --- OPTIONAL: logging ---
# One JSON object per line on stdout. DEBUG, INFO (default), WARNING or ERROR; anything
# else falls back to INFO. It sets this app's own loggers only: third-party libraries
# (httpx, openai, ...) always log at WARNING and above, because their lower levels print
# URLs, headers and prompts. Logs carry ids, counts, error codes and exception types --
# never request bodies, keys, resume/JD text or email -- and exceptions are logged
# without their messages.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- OPTIONAL: error reporting (Sentry) ---
# Off unless SENTRY_DSN is set. With it unset the Sentry SDK is never even imported and nothing
# leaves the server. The DSN is the project's client key: Sentry -> Project Settings -> Client
# Keys (DSN). It must look like https://<key>@<host>/<project-id>; anything else stops the API
# from starting, and the error names this variable and never prints its value. Never commit a
# real one.
#
# Reported: an exception nothing handled, an API error answered with a 5xx (never a 4xx, which
# is the API telling a caller what was wrong), and each failed tick of a background worker
# (one issue per worker and error type; repeated failures are thinned out, so a crash loop is
# a handful of events, not thousands).
# Sent: the error's type and stack frames, the request's method and URL path without its query
# string (the path can include the ids of the records involved), a few ordinary headers
# (including the browser's User-Agent and the Origin), the route, request id or worker name as
# tags, and the error's message except as listed below.
# Not sent: request bodies, query strings, cookies and all other headers, local variables, any
# user email or address, the machine's hostname, and anything from log lines or outbound HTTP
# calls. Text that is left (exception messages, URLs, tags) has the shapes of known secrets --
# JWTs, bearer tokens, API keys, Telegram bot tokens, emails -- replaced; that is a safety net,
# not a guarantee, so see src/between_jobs/api/sentry_scrub.py before relying on it. The
# messages of database, validation, HTTP and AI-provider errors, and of the API's own error
# answers, are not sent at all.
# A Sentry that is down or unreachable never fails a request or a worker.
# If you run a public service, turning this on means checking that its privacy policy's Sentry
# entry (web/src/content/legal.ts) says what is true of your deployment.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# The environment events are filed under. Default production, which is what a deployment that
# forgot to set it should be: reported where an alert for production would see it. Name a
# staging or local run something else. Letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# The share of requests Sentry records as performance traces, from 0 to 1. Default 0: tracing
# is off. Anything else stops the API from starting.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0

# --- OPTIONAL: background workers ---
# The API runs five workers in its own process (outbox, job registry poller, saved-search
# matcher, Gmail reply checker, hiring-signal cache purge). Each runs unless its DISABLE_*
# variable is set to ANY non-empty value. The check is "is it set", not "is it true":
# DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=false and DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=0 both disable the worker. Leave a
# line out (or empty) to keep that worker running. The test suite sets all five. The cache
# purge worker also runs the daily Telegram webhook probe (see below), so switching it off
# switches the probe off too.
# DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=
# DISABLE_JOB_REGISTRY_POLLER=
# DISABLE_SAVED_SEARCH_MATCHER=
# DISABLE_GMAIL_REPLY_CHECKER=
# DISABLE_HIRING_SIGNAL_CACHE_PURGE=
# Not a worker: turns the whole Hiring signals feature off (its routes answer 404
# FEATURE_DISABLED). Same rule -- any non-empty value.
# DISABLE_HIRING_SIGNALS=
# Not a worker either: limits Hiring signals to the people you name. A comma-separated list of
# user ids (the UUIDs in Supabase's auth.users), no empty entries. Leave it out (or empty) and
# everyone may use the feature. When it is set, anyone else -- and anyone who is not signed in --
# gets the same 404 FEATURE_DISABLED the switch above gives, so for them the feature does not
# exist, and the web app hides its menu entries and panels. A malformed list stops the API from
# starting; the error names this variable and never its value.
# HIRING_SIGNALS_ALLOWED_USER_IDS=

# --- OPTIONAL: worker healthchecks (Healthchecks.io, or a compatible service) ---
# A dead-man's switch per background worker. /health tells a platform or UptimeRobot whether
# the API is up, but only while someone is asking; a check here tells you when a worker has
# gone quiet. After each successful tick a worker sends one GET to its check's ping URL, and
# the check alerts when the pings stop for longer than its grace time. Each worker is named by
# its DISABLE_* flag: DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER -> HEALTHCHECKS_URL_OUTBOX_WORKER, and so on.
# Unset means no check for that worker. The value is the ping URL itself (https, with a path,
# no credentials, query or fragment); anything else stops the API from starting, naming the
# variable and never its value. The URL is a credential (anyone who has it can mark the check
# up): never commit a real one. Mind the names: the outbox's setting ends in _WORKER
# (HEALTHCHECKS_URL_OUTBOX_WORKER) even though /health calls that worker "outbox". A setting
# whose name looks like one of these but is none of them is ignored and logged as a warning at
# boot, naming it (never its value); a check that never gets a ping never alerts.
#
# A worker pings at most once per 30 seconds, with a 5 second timeout and no retries, and a
# failed ping is logged at most once a minute and otherwise ignored. A worker that is failing
# tick after tick says so (the check's /fail address) from its third failure in a row; one
# failure its retry cures says nothing. Suggested check settings, for a period and grace that
# fit the worker's interval: outbox (every 5 s, pings every 30 s) period 1 minute, grace 3
# minutes; job registry poller and Gmail reply checker (every 15 min) period 15 minutes,
# grace 45 minutes; hiring-signal cache purge (hourly) period 1 hour, grace 3 hours; saved-
# search matcher (every 6 h) period 6 hours, grace 18 hours.
#
# A worker that is switched off, or that is standing by without its lease (another copy of
# the API holds it), never pings, so its check would go down (once it has been pinged at least
# once; a check never pinged stays "new" and never alerts). Do not create a check for a worker
# you disable. A URL left set for a disabled worker is ignored and logged as a warning.
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_OUTBOX_WORKER=
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_JOB_REGISTRY_POLLER=
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_SAVED_SEARCH_MATCHER=
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_GMAIL_REPLY_CHECKER=
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_HIRING_SIGNAL_CACHE_PURGE=

# --- OPTIONAL: Telegram webhook probe ---
# Telegram stops delivering messages silently when the webhook's address is wrong or
# unreachable or answers errors, and nothing in this service's logs shows it, because nothing
# arrives. On a server with a Telegram bot (TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN), about once a day -- and again
# right after the API starts -- the API asks Telegram itself (getWebhookInfo) and decides:
#   problem  the bot has no webhook set, or the webhook's address does not end in
#            /telegram/webhook (the route this API serves: Telegram is delivering to something
#            else), or the bot is subscribed to a list of update types that leaves out
#            `message` or `callback_query` (a missing or empty list is Telegram's default and is
#            fine), or more than 10 updates are waiting for delivery, or Telegram reports a
#            delivery or synchronization error from the last 25 hours (a synchronization error
#            is Telegram-side, between its own datacenters: nothing to fix, it ages out);
#   ok       none of those, and every field it reads was present and well-formed;
#   unknown  the answer was missing, malformed or never came (Telegram unreachable, a bad bot
#            token): it is tried again at the next hourly tick, up to three times, then the
#            next day.
# The probe runs inside the hiring-signal cache purge worker (so it needs that worker on) and
# is off on a server with no bot. A problem is logged as one WARNING with short reason codes
# (never the webhook's address or the bot token), shown in /health under `telegram_webhook`
# (which never changes /health's status code), and NOT sent to Sentry. It can only see a
# delivery error after Telegram has tried to deliver something: a dead webhook address on a
# bot nobody has messaged since shows nothing yet. It does not know this server's public
# address either, so a webhook re-pointed at another live service on this API's own path (a dev
# copy of the API on another host, say) still reads ok: check the host Telegram has with
# `python scripts/set_telegram_webhook.py --info`.
# With no URL below the probe still runs and logs. With one, it pings that Healthchecks check:
# a plain ping when ok, the check's /fail address on a problem, nothing when unknown -- so the
# check's own period and grace raise the alarm when the pings stop, which is the right alarm
# when nobody can tell. Same rules as the worker settings above: an https URL with a path and
# no credentials, query or fragment (anything else stops the API from starting, naming this
# variable and never its value), and it is a credential: never commit a real one. Suggested
# check settings: period 1 day, grace 6 hours. Do not create the check on a server without a
# bot, or with DISABLE_HIRING_SIGNAL_CACHE_PURGE set: it would go down (a URL left set there is
# ignored and logged as a warning at boot).
# Then confirm the check is armed. A new Healthchecks check stays in its "new" state, which
# never alerts, until it receives its first ping, so a missing ping only alerts once the check
# has been pinged at least once. After setting the URL, restart the API (the probe runs right
# after it starts) or send one manual GET to the ping URL, and confirm in Healthchecks that the
# check turns green (up) before you rely on it. If it stays "new" after the first probe, the
# probe is not getting an answer from Telegram: read the log WARNING and /health.
# HEALTHCHECKS_URL_TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK=

# /health reports each worker -- 503 when an enabled one has died, gone stale (no finished
# tick for 3x its interval) or failed every tick for 30 minutes (60 seconds for the outbox)
# -- and probes Supabase, the resume engine and the LaTeX service. The probes are
# informational: they never change the status code. Any non-empty value here skips them
# (they then read "not_checked"), like the worker flags above. The test suite sets it.
# DISABLE_HEALTH_DEPENDENCY_CHECKS=

# --- One worker instance, enforced ---
# The job registry poller, the saved-search matcher and the Gmail reply checker each hold
# a lease in the database, so only one running copy of this API does their work at a time
# -- an old and a new container overlapping in a deploy, or a second replica, would
# otherwise each do all of it (and spend your LLM credit twice). Apply the migrations
# first: without the lease functions those three workers report `lease_unknown` and
# /health answers 503, on purpose.
#   on  (default)  hold a lease
#   off            no lease. ONLY for a single local process or a deliberate
#                  single-container deployment; nothing stops a second copy.
# Anything else stops the API from starting.
# WORKER_LEASES=on

# --- OPTIONAL: tester programme ---
# Whether the costly features are closed until a person has accepted the tester agreement on
# the enrollment page (/enroll in the web app): every route that carries a per-user rate limit
# (discovery, resume generation, research, PDF export, saving a provider key, ...), the browser
# extension's Draft answer and the Telegram bot's resume generation. A person who has not gets
# a 403 ENROLLMENT_REQUIRED that says to join. Cheap reads, editing your own data and deleting
# your account stay open. If the server cannot check enrollment it refuses the request (a
# retryable 503) rather than let it through.
#   off (default)  nothing is closed; the programme is optional, and a visitor who joins
#                  anyway is only recorded.
#   on             require it.
# Anything else stops the API from starting. Background work (saved-search matching, Gmail
# reply checking) is not affected, so a person who has not joined or has withdrawn still has it
# until they pause, delete or disconnect it; the Tester Agreement says so.
# TESTER_PROGRAM_REQUIRED=off

# --- OPTIONAL: scoring prompt override ---
# Path to a plain-text file that replaces the built-in system prompt for batch job-fit
# scoring (Discover). Read on every scoring call. An unset variable, or a file that is
# missing, unreadable or blank, falls back to the built-in prompt. The reply must keep
# the JSON shape the built-in prompt asks for.
# JOB_SCORING_SYSTEM_PROMPT_PATH=

# --- Container only ---
# PORT is read by the Docker image's start command, not by the Python code: the port
# uvicorn listens on inside the container (default 8000). A platform sets its own.
# PORT=8000
# RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_ID is set by the Railway platform and used only as a label in the
# worker-lease holder id; it is never needed anywhere else. Do not set it yourself.
# RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_ID=
# RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA is likewise set by Railway: the commit being run. When error reporting
# is on it names the release events belong to. Optional; a value that is not a plain identifier
# is ignored. Set it yourself on another platform if you want releases told apart.
# RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA=

# --- Operator scripts only (scripts/); the API never reads these ---
# Do NOT put the first two scripts' variables in .env: those scripts deliberately do not
# load it, so a stale file cannot point them at the wrong project. Export them in the shell
# for one run.
#
# scripts/copy_registry_sample.py copies a sample of the job registry from one Supabase
# project (read only) to another (written). Source and target are explicit:
# SAMPLE_SOURCE_SUPABASE_URL=
# SAMPLE_SOURCE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# SAMPLE_TARGET_SUPABASE_URL=
# SAMPLE_TARGET_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# scripts/delete_account.py takes SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY from the
# shell too (and requires --confirm-ref), for the same reason.
#
# scripts/sign_and_publish_ats_field_map.py signs the browser extension's per-ATS field
# maps. The private signing key (base64 Ed25519) lives OUTSIDE this repository and is
# never given to the running API, which only reads maps that were already signed.
# Unlike the two scripts above, this one does call load_dotenv(), but keep the key out of
# .env anyway. Pass --key-file (a path outside the repository) or export the variable for
# one run. If the variable is set it WINS over --key-file, so unset it when you mean to
# use the file.
# ATS_FIELD_MAP_SIGNING_KEY=
